-- ============================================================
-- roc4: this part's cases seat text, unchanged (merged by concatenation)
-- ============================================================
-- Rules of Court 2021 (Singapore), Order 19: cases for the seat layer (part roc4).
-- The text is a converter rendering of Singapore Statutes Online, not the official text.
-- Each case is a set of facts a person in the appeal would state, and the answer the TEXT gives, written by hand from the text before the run.
-- Dates: the example judgment is dated 27 January 2026, so "14 days after" has a last day of 10 February 2026, taken here as the user's own answer
-- (Order 3, rule 3 does the counting; this part receives its result as the input "my last day").
-- Every case asks a question exactly as the seat layer asks it.  NOTHING is asserted where the text does not answer.

-- ===== Division 1 -- General (rules 1 to 12) =====

-- ===== Rule 1 -- scope of this Order =====
-- A defendant in a Magistrate's Court action appeals against the judgment given at the trial of an originating claim: paragraph (a)(i).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The same, in the District Court, after damages were assessed by a District Judge: paragraph (a)(ii).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `District Court` `A District Judge` `o 19 r 3(b) after damages are assessed or accounts are taken` `Originating claim` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Damages assessed by someone other than a District Judge are not named in paragraph (a)(ii): the text does not answer.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `District Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(b) after damages are assessed or accounts are taken` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- A committal order for contempt in the Magistrate's Court: paragraph (a)(iii).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(c) an application for a committal order for contempt of court` `Originating application` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A trial of an originating application in the District Court is not named in paragraph (a)(i), which says "originating claim": the text does not answer.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `District Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating application` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- A judgment of a Judge of the General Division: paragraph (b).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `General Division` `A Judge` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A judgment of the Registrar of the Supreme Court: paragraph (b) says "except", so the text answers no.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `General Division` `The Registrar` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A judgment of the Appellate Division: paragraph (c).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a judgment` `Appellate Division` `A Judge` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- An appeal from a tribunal to the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal under a written law: paragraph (d).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal from a tribunal` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A tribunal appeal that is not to the Appellate Division or the Court of Appeal: paragraph (d) is limited by its own wording.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal from a tribunal` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- An application to the appellate Court relating to such an appeal: paragraph (e).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an application to the appellate Court relating to an appeal` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- An application that does not relate to such an appeal: paragraph (e) does not reach it.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an application to the appellate Court relating to an appeal` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- An appeal against a Disciplinary Tribunal decision under the Medical Registration Act: paragraph (f).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat an appeal against a Disciplinary Tribunal decision under the Medical Registration Act` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Something else: rule 1 lists six kinds of matter without saying "only", so the text does not answer.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 1, does Order 19 apply to my matter` `o 19 seat something else` `Magistrate's Court` `A Magistrate` `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` `Originating claim` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING

-- ===== Rule 2 -- general matters and structure =====
-- District Court to the General Division: Division 2 (paragraph (2)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `District Court` `General Division` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 Division 2`
-- Magistrate's Court to the General Division: Division 2.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `Magistrate's Court` `General Division` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 Division 2`
-- General Division to the Appellate Division: Division 3 (paragraph (3)(a)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `General Division` `Appellate Division` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 Division 3`
-- General Division to the Court of Appeal (an appeal transferred): Division 3.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `General Division` `Court of Appeal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 Division 3`
-- Appellate Division to the Court of Appeal: Division 3 (paragraph (3)(c)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `Appellate Division` `Court of Appeal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 Division 3`
-- A tribunal to the Appellate Division: Division 3 (paragraph (3)(b)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `General Division` `Appellate Division` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 Division 3`
-- A Disciplinary Tribunal under the Medical Registration Act to the General Division: Division 4 (paragraph (4)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `General Division` `General Division` TRUE TRUE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 Division 4`
-- A route the rule does not name (Family Division to the Court of Appeal): the rule names three routes without saying "only".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2, which Division of Order 19 deals with my appeal` `Family Division` `Court of Appeal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `o 19 r 2 no Division is named for the route`
-- A written law deals with the right to appeal: the Order is subject to it.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2(1), does a written law on the right to appeal or on permission to appeal take priority over this Order` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- No such written law: paragraph (1) does not say what follows.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 2(1), does a written law on the right to appeal or on permission to appeal take priority over this Order` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

-- ===== Rule 3 -- definitions =====
-- Given by the lower Court at a trial: a "judgment" ("means", paragraph (a)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is the decision I am appealing against a "judgment" for the purposes of Order 19` TRUE `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Given by the lower Court in an application for a prerogative order: paragraph (d).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is the decision I am appealing against a "judgment" for the purposes of Order 19` TRUE `o 19 r 3(d) an application for a prerogative order` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Given by the lower Court in an appeal against one of those: paragraph (e).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is the decision I am appealing against a "judgment" for the purposes of Order 19` TRUE `o 19 r 3(e) an appeal against a judgment described in paragraphs (a) to (d)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Given by the lower Court, but in a kind of decision the definition does not list: "means" closes the list.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is the decision I am appealing against a "judgment" for the purposes of Order 19` TRUE `o 19 r 3 any other decision` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not given by the lower Court: the definition is of a judgment "given by the lower Court".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is the decision I am appealing against a "judgment" for the purposes of Order 19` FALSE `o 19 r 3(a) a trial` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The notice of appeal is named in the definition of "record of appeal".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of appeal` `o 19 r 3 the notice of appeal` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The order granting permission to appeal is named.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of appeal` `o 19 r 3 the order granting permission to appeal` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The certificate for security for costs is named.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of appeal` `o 19 r 3 the certificate for security for costs` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A document filed in the lower Court that is relevant to the matter decided and the nature of the appeal.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of appeal` `o 19 r 3 another document filed in the lower Court` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The same, but not relevant to the matter decided and the nature of the appeal: the definition says "so far as are relevant".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of appeal` `o 19 r 3 another document filed in the lower Court` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A document of no listed kind: the definition "means" those and no others.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of appeal` `o 19 r 3 any other document` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A certified copy of the judgment or grounds of decision of the lower Court is named in the definition of "core bundle of documents".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of a core bundle of documents` `o 19 r 3 a certified copy of the judgment or grounds of decision of the lower Court` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The extracted order of the lower Court is named.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of a core bundle of documents` `o 19 r 3 the extracted order of the lower Court` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- An index cross-referencing each document to the record of appeal is named.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of a core bundle of documents` `o 19 r 3 an index cross-referencing each document to the record of appeal or supplemental record of appeal` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A notice of appeal is not named: the definition "means" the four items.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of a core bundle of documents` `o 19 r 3 the notice of appeal` EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The extracted order of the lower Court is named in the definition of "record of proceedings".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of proceedings` `o 19 r 3 the extracted order of the lower Court` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A certified copy of the judgment or grounds of decision of the lower Court is named.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of proceedings` `o 19 r 3 a certified copy of the judgment or grounds of decision of the lower Court` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The certified transcript of the hearing is named, in an appeal from the General Division, the District Court or the Magistrate's Court.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of proceedings` `o 19 r 3 the certified transcript of the official record of hearing taken at the hearing of the cause or matter` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The certified transcript in any other appeal (a tribunal appeal) is not within the definition.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of proceedings` `o 19 r 3 the certified transcript of the official record of hearing taken at the hearing of the cause or matter` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The notice of appeal is not named.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 3, is this document part of the record of proceedings` `o 19 r 3 the notice of appeal` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- ===== Rule 4 -- when time for appeal starts to run =====
-- Costs were decided at the same hearing on 5 January 2026: time starts to run after 5 January 2026 (paragraph (1)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4, from what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST (Date 5 1 2026)
-- Costs were decided 15 days later, within the 30 days of paragraph (1A): time starts after 20 January 2026.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4, from what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 20 1 2026) (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST (Date 20 1 2026)
-- Costs were still undecided on 10 March 2026: the 30 days after 5 January 2026 ended on 4 February 2026 and time starts after that (paragraph (1A)).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4, from what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal run` (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST (Date 4 2 2026)
-- Costs were decided only on 20 February 2026, after the 30 days: still 4 February 2026.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4, from what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 20 2 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST (Date 4 2 2026)
-- Costs are undecided on 20 January 2026, still within the 30 days: the text gives no start date yet.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4, from what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal run` (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 20 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- A written law provides otherwise: paragraph (1) is "Subject to any written law", which is not among the sources.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4, from what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- The Court has ordered otherwise: the order is not among the sources.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4, from what date does the time for filing my notice of appeal run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Asked on 4 January 2026, before the lower Court had decided on 5 January 2026: time has not started.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 4 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Asked on 5 January 2026 itself: the time does not start "until after" that day.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Asked on 6 January 2026, costs having been decided on 5 January 2026: paragraph (1) says only when time does not start, and (1A) does not apply, so it does not say that it has started.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 6 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Costs still undecided, asked on 4 February 2026, the 30th day after 5 January 2026: the 30 days have not expired, so the bar still holds.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 4 2 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Costs still undecided, asked on 5 February 2026, the 31st day: paragraph (1A) says time starts to run after the expiry of the 30-day period, so it has started.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 5 2 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The same day with a written law that provides otherwise: paragraph (1A) is not made subject to a written law, so it has started.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 5 2 2026) TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Costs decided on 20 February 2026, after the 30 days, asked on 10 March 2026: time started after 4 February 2026.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 20 2 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Costs decided on 20 February 2026, asked on 20 January 2026: it has not started.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), has the time for filing my notice of appeal started to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 20 2 2026) (Date 20 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Asked on 4 January 2026, before the lower Court had decided: the bar holds.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), is it too early for the time for filing my notice of appeal to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 4 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Asked on 5 January 2026 itself: "until after" that day, so the bar still holds.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), is it too early for the time for filing my notice of appeal to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 5 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Asked on 6 January 2026: the bar has ended.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), is it too early for the time for filing my notice of appeal to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 6 1 2026) FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The Court has ordered otherwise ("unless the Court otherwise orders"): the bar does not hold.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), is it too early for the time for filing my notice of appeal to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 4 1 2026) FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- A written law provides otherwise: not among the sources.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 4(1), is it too early for the time for filing my notice of appeal to run` (Date 5 1 2026) TRUE (Date 5 1 2026) (Date 4 1 2026) TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

-- ===== Rule 5 -- permission to intervene =====
-- A person who is not a party in the appeal may apply.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(1), may I apply to intervene in the appeal` FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A person who is already a party: paragraph (1) speaks only of a person who is not a party.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(1), may I apply to intervene in the appeal` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Application and affidavit both filed and both served on all parties with an interest.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(2), have I filed and served my application to intervene as required` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The affidavit was not filed.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(2), have I filed and served my application to intervene as required` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Not served on all parties who have an interest.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(2), have I filed and served my application to intervene as required` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The application itself was not filed.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(2), have I filed and served my application to intervene as required` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The affidavit sets out the applicant's interest.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(3), does my affidavit have to set out my interest in the appeal` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The affidavit does not set out the applicant's interest: paragraph (3) says it "must".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(3), does my affidavit have to set out my interest in the appeal` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Permission to intervene has been granted: the Court may impose conditions.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(4), may the Court make my permission to intervene subject to conditions` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Permission has not been granted: "when the appellate Court grants permission" is not met.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(4), may the Court make my permission to intervene subject to conditions` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Granted, and the condition is security for costs: named in "including".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(4), may the Court order me, as an intervener, to give security for costs` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Permission not granted: no condition can be imposed.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(4), may the Court order me, as an intervener, to give security for costs` FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Granted, but the condition is something other than security for costs: "including" names one condition and leaves the list open.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 5(4), may the Court order me, as an intervener, to give security for costs` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

-- ===== Rule 6 -- stay of enforcement, etc. =====
-- No direction either way: "an appeal does not operate as a stay". The answer to "does my appeal stop enforcement" is no.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 6(1), does my appeal stop enforcement of the lower Court's decision` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The lower Court or the appellate Court has directed otherwise: what the direction says is not in the Rules.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 6(1), does my appeal stop enforcement of the lower Court's decision` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- No direction: "no intermediate act or proceeding is to be invalidated by an appeal". The answer is no.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 6(2), does my appeal invalidate an intermediate act or proceeding` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The appellate Court has directed otherwise: not in the Rules.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 6(2), does my appeal invalidate an intermediate act or proceeding` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Enforcement was delayed by the appeal and no one has ordered otherwise: interest "is to be allowed".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 6(3), is interest allowed for the time enforcement was delayed by the appeal` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The lower Court or the appellate Court has ordered otherwise: "unless" the Court otherwise orders, so the text answers no.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 6(3), is interest allowed for the time enforcement was delayed by the appeal` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- No time for which enforcement was delayed: "for such time as enforcement has been delayed" is a period the text fixes in its own words, and none is left (fork F10).
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 6(3), is interest allowed for the time enforcement was delayed by the appeal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- ===== Rule 7 -- powers of the appellate Court =====
-- A document to be served on a non-party to the appeal.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(1), may the appellate Court order a party to serve a document on a non-party` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A document for a party: paragraph (1) speaks only of service on a non-party.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(1), may the appellate Court order a party to serve a document on a non-party` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- A non-party to the appeal may be allowed or invited to give its views.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(2), may the appellate Court allow or invite me, as a non-party, to give my views` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- A party: paragraph (2) speaks only of a non-party.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(2), may the appellate Court allow or invite me, as a non-party, to give my views` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- The oral submission is one the appellate Court has ordered.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(3), am I allowed to make an oral submission at the hearing of the appeal` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- An oral submission the Court has not ordered: "only such oral submissions as the appellate Court orders".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(3), am I allowed to make an oral submission at the hearing of the appeal` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- An order relating to a part of the decision of the lower Court, though not the subject of any appeal.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(4), may the appellate Court make an order on a part of the decision that is not the subject of any appeal` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- An order not about the lower Court's decision: paragraph (4) does not reach it.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(4), may the appellate Court make an order on a part of the decision that is not the subject of any appeal` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- The only reason is that no one appealed against an earlier order: "not restricted by reason only" of that. The answer to "is it restricted" is no.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(5), is the appellate Court's power to decide the merits restricted because there was no appeal against an earlier order` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Some other reason: the paragraph says nothing of other reasons.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(5), is the appellate Court's power to decide the merits restricted because there was no appeal against an earlier order` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Substantial injustice will be caused otherwise: a new trial may be ordered.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(6), may the appellate Court order a new trial` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- No substantial injustice: "only if".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(6), may the appellate Court order a new trial` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- No written law restricts it.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(7), does the appellate Court have power to receive further evidence` FALSE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- "Subject to any written law": which law, and what it says, is not in the Rules.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(7), does the appellate Court have power to receive further evidence` TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Evidence of matters after the date of the decision appealed against is excepted from the bar; the paragraph says nothing more about it.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(7), may further evidence be given on the appeal` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Not about later matters, but there are special grounds.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(7), may further evidence be given on the appeal` FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Neither: "no such further evidence ... may be given except on special grounds".
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(7), may further evidence be given on the appeal` FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Adduced in the manner the appellate Court directed.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(8), is the further evidence adduced as required` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Not adduced in that manner: it "must" be.
#ASSERT `Under Order 19, rule 7(8), is the further evidence adduced as required` FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE

-- ===== Rule 8 -- absence of parties =====
